  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Task: There is an integer array `nums` sorted in ascending order (with **distinct** values).\n\nPrior to being passed to your function, `nums` is **possibly left rotated** at an unknown index `k` (`1 <= k < nums.length`) such that the resulting array is `[nums[k], nums[k+1], ..., nums[n-1], nums[0], nums[1], ..., nums[k-1]]` (**0-indexed**). As an example, `[0,1,2,4,5,6,7]` might be left rotated by `3` indices and become `[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]`.\n\nYou receive the array `nums` **after** the possible rotation and an integer `target`, output *the index of* `target` *if it is in* `nums`*, or* `-1` *if it is not in* `nums`.\n\nYou must write an algorithm with `O(log n)` runtime complexity.",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 5000",
      "-10^4 <= nums[i], target <= 10^4",
      "nums are unique",
      "nums is a rotated sorted array"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Single line: JSON array of all arguments, e.g. [arg1, arg2, ...]",
    "outputFormat": "Single line: JSON-serialised return value",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
